This is a sample entry for checking the journal’s typography. Read it on a small screen, then a larger one: the same words should have enough room to breathe in both places.

## Paying attention

An ordinary paragraph can carry **a strong emphasis**, *a quieter emphasis*, or ***both at once***. Sometimes we ~~reach for a conclusion~~ stay with a question. A [link to About](/about/) should feel at home in the sentence; an [external link](https://example.com/) should be equally readable.

A short line ends here.\
The next begins without starting a new paragraph.

{% marginNote title="a margin title?" %}
This margin note belongs beside the writing on wide screens and returns to the reading flow on smaller ones. It can include **emphasis** and a [link](/about/).
{% /marginNote %}

### Things noticed on the walk

- A patch of light moving across the wall.
- A familiar sound heard differently.
  - A passing bicycle.
  - A cup set down on a table.
- A thought that did not need finishing.

### A small practice

1. Stop for a moment.
1. Name one thing you can see.
   1. Notice its shape.
   1. Notice what surrounds it.
1. Continue without trying to keep the moment.

> Attention is sometimes less a matter of looking harder than of allowing something to remain unfamiliar.
> 
> A second paragraph lets us check the space within a quotation.

## Notes from the desk

Inline code such as `const attention = "here"` should remain distinct inside a paragraph. A longer example needs its own space:

```javascript
function notice(detail) {
  return `Today I noticed ${detail}.`;
}

console.log(notice("the afternoon light"));
```

